{"id":"gotcha-example-001","type":"gotcha","fact":"Example: Truthy check on numeric values fails for explicit zero - if (value) is false when value === 0.","recommendation":"Use !== undefined or !== null instead of truthy checks when the value could legitimately be 0 or empty string.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"coderabbit","reference":"PR review","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["javascript","type-coercion","validation"],"affectedFiles":["**/*.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"gotcha-pin-shellcheck-version","type":"gotcha","fact":"GitHub-hosted runners ship a preinstalled ShellCheck whose version drifts; different versions report different findings (SC2236 fired on the runner but not on local 0.11.0).","recommendation":"Pin ShellCheck in CI by downloading the official koalaman release; keep SHELLCHECK_VERSION in ci.yml in sync with local dev (currently v0.11.0).","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"discovered fixing CI in PR #11","date":"2026-05-18"}],"tags":["ci","shellcheck","lint"],"affectedFiles":[".github/workflows/ci.yml"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-18T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-18T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id": "gotcha-certbot-latest-flag-drift", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "The container is built FROM certbot/certbot:latest, so certbot's CLI drifts with each rebuild. Two flags broke: --manual-public-ip-logging-ok was removed (certbot errors 'unrecognized arguments'), and the bare --dns-ionos plugin selector became an ambiguous prefix of --dns-ionos-credentials/--dns-ionos-propagation-seconds ('ambiguous option: --dns-ionos').", "recommendation": "Select third-party DNS plugins with '--authenticator dns-<name>' (unambiguous), never the bare '--dns-<name>' selector (that convenience flag only exists for certbot's official plugins). Avoid deprecated certbot flags. Consider pinning the certbot image to a known tag instead of :latest to stop silent CLI drift on rebuild.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "fixing certbot failures production", "date": "2026-06-09"}], "tags": ["certbot", "docker", "dns-ionos", "flags", "letsencrypt"], "affectedFiles": ["commands/proxy.sh", "Dockerfile"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-06-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-06-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-reinstall-breaks-bind-mount", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "easy proxy create bind-mounts ${EASY_DIR}/easyhome -> /usr/local/share/easy (EASY_DIR = dirname of the resolved 'easy' binary, i.e. the npm install dir). Running 'npm install -g' while the proxy container is up rewrites that directory, so the live container's bind mount points at the replaced inode and nginx -s reload fails: open() \"/usr/local/share/easy/nginx.conf\" failed (No such file or directory).", "recommendation": "After upgrading the CLI with npm install -g, recreate the container so it re-binds to the fresh easyhome: 'easy proxy destroy && easy proxy create' (or 'easy proxy restart', which re-resolves the bind source). A plain reload is not enough. If backends live on Docker networks, set EASY_PROXY_NETWORK or run 'easy proxy recover' after recreating.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "reload failure production after upgrading to 2.3.1", "date": "2026-06-09"}], "tags": ["docker", "bind-mount", "npm", "reload", "nginx", "upgrade"], "affectedFiles": ["commands/proxy.sh", "easy"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-06-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-06-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-static-upstream-blocks-nginx-startup", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "An 'upstream { server <hostname>; }' block resolves the hostname ONCE, when nginx parses the config. If it fails, nginx aborts with '[emerg] host not found in upstream' and NO vhost starts - one dead backend takes down every site on the proxy. In Docker a stopped container is not resolvable by the embedded DNS, so this is routine, not exotic. Critically, it does NOT fire on 'nginx -s reload' (the master rejects the config and old workers keep serving), only on a cold start: easy proxy destroy && create, docker restart, dockerd restart, host reboot - which is why the mine can sit armed for months. A literal hostname in proxy_pass (no variables) has the exact same startup-resolution behaviour even outside an upstream block.", "recommendation": "In generated and hand-written vhosts prefer a request-time target: 'set $u http://<host>:<port>;' at server level plus 'proxy_pass $u;', with 'resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=30s;'. A dead backend then yields 502 on that site alone and nginx still boots. Path handling is unchanged as long as neither form carries a URI part. Accept that this loses upstream 'keepalive': in open-source nginx you cannot have both (the 'resolve' parameter on an upstream server line is NGINX Plus only). Leave upstream blocks alone when the target is a literal IP - there is no name to resolve, so no startup risk.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production session production: first working IONOS DNS-01 integration + auto-renewal", "date": "2026-09-09"}], "tags": ["nginx", "docker", "dns", "upstream", "resolver", "outage"], "affectedFiles": ["easyhome/templates/*.conf", "commands/proxy.sh"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-certbot-renew-fails-on-orphan-lineage", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "'certbot renew' walks EVERY file in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal/ and exits non-zero if any single lineage fails or fails to parse, even when the certificate you care about renewed fine. Two real cases hit this: a lineage with 'authenticator = manual' (cannot run non-interactively: 'An authentication script must be provided with --manual-auth-hook'), and a stale conf carrying absolute paths from a previous, non-containerised install ('expected /path/live/<d>/cert.pem to be a symlink'). Any systemd unit or cron job wrapping the command then reports failure on every run, permanently - destroying the only signal that would warn you when a renewal you depend on actually breaks.", "recommendation": "Audit 'docker exec easy-proxy ls /etc/letsencrypt/renewal/' before trusting a renewal timer. Park unrenewable or stale lineages in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal.disabled/ (mv, never 'certbot delete' - moving keeps the cert files in live/ and is reversible), then confirm 'certbot renew --dry-run' exits clean. The planned 'easy proxy renew' command should surface per-lineage status instead of propagating a single aggregate exit code.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production session production: first working IONOS DNS-01 integration + auto-renewal", "date": "2026-09-09"}], "tags": ["certbot", "letsencrypt", "renewal", "systemd", "monitoring"], "affectedFiles": ["commands/proxy.sh"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-certbot-ionos-rewrites-credentials-file", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "easy proxy certbot-ionos regenerates /etc/letsencrypt/ionos.ini from scratch on every invocation (commands/proxy.sh:151, heredoc with '>'), sourcing pass/env each time. Because EASY_LETSENCRYPT_DIR is bind-mounted to /etc/letsencrypt, the file is directly editable from the host - and any hand edit is silently destroyed by the next run of the command.", "recommendation": "Treat pass (ionos/api-key, ionos/api-secret) and IONOS_API_ENDPOINT as the source of truth and change credentials there, not in ionos.ini. To manage the file by hand, bypass the subcommand and call certbot directly: docker exec easy-proxy certbot certonly --non-interactive --agree-tos --email \"$EASY_LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL\" --authenticator dns-ionos --dns-ionos-credentials /etc/letsencrypt/ionos.ini -d <d> -d '*.<d>' (also the way to add --dry-run, which the subcommand does not expose).", "confidence": "medium", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production session production: first working IONOS DNS-01 integration + auto-renewal", "date": "2026-09-09"}], "tags": ["ionos", "certbot", "credentials", "idempotency"], "affectedFiles": ["commands/proxy.sh"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-generated-catchalls-collide-with-vhosts", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "The generated catch-alls http.default.conf / https.default.conf declare both '<domain>' and '*.<domain>'. A hand-written vhost for the same apex on the same port produces 'nginx: [warn] conflicting server name \"<domain>\" on 0.0.0.0:443, ignored' - a warning, not an error, so nginx starts and one of the two blocks is silently dropped. The winner is decided by the alphabetical expansion of 'include /domains/*/*.conf' in easyhome/nginx.conf, so renaming a file can silently swap which vhost serves the apex. Separately, nginx server_name precedence is exact > wildcard > regex, so a '*.<domain>' catch-all always beats a regex block like 'server_name ~^(?<subdomain>\\w+)\\.<domain>$' no matter which file loads first - such a regex vhost is dead code.", "recommendation": "Treat a 'conflicting server name' warning as a real defect, not noise: decide which file owns the name and remove it from the other's server_name. Never rely on include order for correctness. When auditing, remember doctor does not detect this - only an actual nginx reload reports it.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production session production: first working IONOS DNS-01 integration + auto-renewal", "date": "2026-09-09"}], "tags": ["nginx", "server_name", "vhost", "templates", "precedence"], "affectedFiles": ["easyhome/templates/*.conf", "easyhome/nginx.conf"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "gotcha-generic-compose-aliases-collide-on-edge-network", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "Docker Compose registers each service name (backend, frontend, db, api...) as a DNS alias on every network the service joins. On a shared edge network (EASY_PROXY_NETWORK) several stacks typically publish the same generic aliases - e.g. two stacks both exposing 'backend', or three databases all answering to 'db'. A container attached to both its private network and the edge network that resolves a generic name (e.g. an app's nginx doing 'proxy_pass http://backend:5001') can reach ANOTHER stack's container - silently, and possibly only after a restart.", "recommendation": "Attach ONLY the proxied entry container to the edge network (an explicit compose override file such as docker-compose.<edge>.yml), keep db/redis/backend on the private network, and make any cross-container reference from that entry container use a unique container_name, never the service alias. Before attaching, list the aliases already present: for c in $(docker network inspect <edge> --format '{{range .Containers}}{{.Name}} {{end}}'); do docker inspect $c --format '{{with index .NetworkSettings.Networks \"<edge>\"}}{{.DNSNames}}{{end}}'; done", "confidence": "high", "tags": ["docker", "networking", "edge-network", "dns"], "affectedFiles": [], "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production operations", "date": "2026-09-10"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z"}
{"id": "gotcha-compose-explicit-f-skips-override", "type": "gotcha", "fact": "docker compose loads docker-compose.override.yml automatically ONLY when no -f is given. Production scripts that run 'docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up' ignore the override entirely, so edge-network wiring placed there never applies in production and the proxy cannot reach the app (nginx answers 502).", "recommendation": "Put proxy wiring in an explicitly named file (e.g. docker-compose.<edge>.yml) and add it to the production -f list; verify with 'docker compose <all -f> config' and 'docker inspect <container>' for the actual network list.", "confidence": "high", "tags": ["docker-compose", "deploy", "edge-network"], "affectedFiles": [], "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production operations", "date": "2026-09-10"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z"}
{"id": "security-never-git-the-letsencrypt-dir", "type": "security", "fact": "EASY_LETSENCRYPT_DIR holds certificate private keys (archive/*/privkey*.pem, keys/), the ACME account key (accounts/*/private_key.json) and DNS-provider credentials (ionos.ini, secret.txt). Putting the whole directory under git - even in a private repo - leaks all of them into history permanently, including the account key that keeps signing future orders.", "recommendation": "Never 'git add -A' in EASY_LETSENCRYPT_DIR. If you want config under version control, use a separate repo with an allowlist (renewal/*.conf, renewal-hooks/) and back up keys, accounts and credential files with encryption instead. If a legacy repo already contains keys, treat the ACME account key as compromised-by-exposure: restrict or delete the remote and consider rotating the account. A repo owned by another user triggers git's 'dubious ownership' error; use 'git -c safe.directory=<dir>' per command instead of a global exception.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production operations", "date": "2026-09-10"}], "tags": ["security", "letsencrypt", "git", "secrets"], "affectedFiles": [], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
